#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""rbtv-ignite-watchdog — CMP-28, ONE bounded pass. Probe · restart · report.

Runs OUTSIDE the daemon's process tree and job queue (a systemd user timer), because
the one thing an in-daemon health loop can never see is the daemon being dead.
Deterministic only: it never escalates to an agent and never widens beyond
probe/restart/report (CMP-28 invariant 2 / PRIN-7). ⚠ AMENDED 2026-08-14 (owner ruling,
daemon-issues §2): the DAEMON row now INTERPRETS its own `down` verdict before acting —
it consults systemd, and withholds the restart when the unit is alive or the strikes are
not spent. See `daemon_restart_gate`. Every other row is unchanged.

Silence means healthy: the owner DM fires ONLY when this pass ACTED or when a
restart did NOT restore the subject (CMP-28 invariant 3). A chatty watchdog trains
its reader to ignore it.

⚠ AMENDED 2026-08-25 (T4-R9, C-17 — the BIT-7 cure). Silence-means-healthy held everywhere
EXCEPT the one case that matters: an outage the identity read could not resolve determinately
produced no note and no record at all, and the 2026-08-19 18:18:44→21:24:59 daemon-down
(3h05m) passed with `restarts: 0` and no owner alarm. Three duties now close that:
  · OUTAGE LEDGER (`ledger()`, RBTV_WATCHDOG_LEDGER) — append-only, one line per decision:
    every restart TAKEN and every restart WITHHELD with its reason, plus every non-healthy
    daemon reading and the recovered outage duration. Survives watchdog and daemon restarts.
  · N-CONSECUTIVE-FAIL ALARM (`daemon_health_streak`) — N = STRIKES non-determinately-healthy
    passes raises ONE alarm even when the restart is withheld, routed through the ONE alarm
    emitter (`ignite/observation/emitter.js` via the sibling `watchdog-alarm.js` shim) into the
    durable outbox. This tool composes no alarm text of its own and posts to no Slack of its own.
  · NON-SLACK DEAD-MAN (`deadman_ping`, RBTV_WATCHDOG_DEADMAN_URL) — a healthchecks-style HTTPS
    ping on every determinately-healthy pass. A MISSED ping is the out-of-band alert, and it is
    the only one that still fires when Slack is unreachable or when this watchdog is itself dead.
    RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_FILE (or --dry-run) makes it structurally incapable of pinging.

⚠ AMENDED 2026-08-28 (wave test 2). An `alarm` verdict now goes to the ONE alarm registry
through `emit_alarm`, and NOT to the owner-DM leg — one delivery, not two (`raise_row_alarm` /
`clear_row_alarm`, and the block above ROW_UNITS for why). Before this, a standing `alarm` reached
only a DM: the registry file did not exist, so the system digest's "Open conditions" and
`ignite status`'s `open_conditions` were both structurally blind to it, and a master seat asked
about standing conditions answered "none" while this tool printed `alarm` every 60 seconds. A row
that reads `up` again CLEARS its own registry row; `--dry-run` raises and clears nothing.

Exit codes — ⚠ AMENDED 2026-08-15 (owner ruling R1): AN ALARM IS NOT A SYSTEMD FAILURE.
  0  the pass RAN. All green, it acted, a subject is still down, an alarm stands — every
     one of those is a completed pass, and the VERDICT is carried by the owner DM and by
     the state file (RBTV_WATCHDOG_STATE), never by the exit code. WHY: the timer re-fires
     every ~60s, so a standing alarm reported as exit 1 logged `Failed with result
     exit-code` once a minute and made `systemctl --user --failed` useless for exactly the
     window something was wrong — and a watchdog correctly alarming became
     indistinguishable at a glance from a watchdog that had crashed.
  2  usage error
  anything else — THE WATCHDOG ITSELF BROKE (an unhandled exception). That is now the ONLY
     thing that paints this unit FAILED, which is what makes a failed unit worth reading.

Environment (every per-instance value is resolved at runtime; nothing is baked in):
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_WORKSPACE     the workspace root — the folder holding the install record
                              .rbtv/modules/ignite/server.json (D27). Default: the CWD, and
                              ONLY when the CWD itself roots that record; otherwise the pass
                              REFUSES with exit 2 rather than invent a workspace. See the
                              block above resolve_workspace() for the outage that cost.
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_GATEWAY       gateway URL          (default http://127.0.0.1:7431/)
  IGNITE_WATCHDOG_TOKEN       the watchdog's OWN minted gateway sender token. No
                              fallback to another sender's token, deliberately:
                              borrowing one would run every probe under the wrong
                              sender id in the gateway's audit columns, and would
                              silently satisfy the mint step the enabling gate
                              exists to force. Absent = alarm, not a quiet pass.
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_DAEMON_UNIT   default rbtv-ignite.service
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_BRIDGE_UNIT   default rbtv-chat-bridge.service
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_PROBE_TIMER   default rbtv-probe-suite.timer
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_TARGETS       comma list to scope the pass (default: all three).
                              The test-override hook, mirroring RBTV_IGNITE_UNIT —
                              a probe points this at a throwaway unit instead of
                              editing the real probe table.
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_OPERATOR      path to rbtv-ignite-daemon (default: resolved as a
                              sibling capability, else on PATH)
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_STATE         alert-dedupe state file (default
                              <workspace>/.rbtv/runtime/watchdog/state.json)
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_DAEMON_STATE  prior-pass daemon identity, the state the RESTARTED /
                              CRASH-LOOP / IDENTITY verdicts compare against (default
                              <workspace>/.rbtv/runtime/watchdog/daemon.json). Its OWN
                              file: the alert-dedupe state above is cleared to null on
                              every all-green pass, which is exactly the pass a restart
                              has to be detected ACROSS.
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_FAILCOUNT     the daemon row's consecutive-failure counter (default
                              <workspace>/.rbtv/runtime/watchdog/failcount.json). Its own
                              file for the same reason daemon.json is: the alert-dedupe
                              state is cleared on every green pass.
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_ROW_ALARMS    which rows currently hold an OPEN row in the alarm registry
                              (default <workspace>/.rbtv/runtime/watchdog/row-alarms.json).
                              Its own file for the same reason as the two above, and it is
                              what makes the emitter call once per EPISODE rather than once
                              per 60s pass.
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_STRIKES       consecutive failed daemon passes required before a restart
                              is allowed at all (default 3)
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_RESTART_BACKOFF_SECONDS  after a daemon restart, how long a still-down
                              verdict is reported rather than re-restarted (default 600)
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_REALERT_SECONDS  how long an UNCHANGED alert stays suppressed
                              before it is re-sent (default 21600 = 6h). Without
                              this ceiling a stuck condition DMs once and then
                              reads exactly like a healthy system forever.
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_FILE   TEST DOUBLE: when set, notifications are appended
                              here as JSON lines and Slack is not called
  RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_PREFIX prepended to every message (used to mark a TEST DM)
  SLACK_BOT_TOKEN             owner-DM notify credential
  IGNITE_CHAT_BRIDGE_CONFIG   the chat bridge's own config — read ONLY to resolve
                              WHO the owner is, so the two components can never
                              disagree about it
"""
import calendar
import hashlib
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import urllib.error
import urllib.request
from pathlib import Path

HELLO_MARK = "Socket Mode connection established (hello)"
RECONNECT_FAIL_MARK = "Socket Mode reconnect failed"
UA = {"content-type": "application/json; charset=utf-8"}


def env(name, default=None):
    v = os.environ.get(name)
    return v if v not in (None, "") else default


# ⚠⚠ A `.rbtv/` THAT DOES NOT ROOT THE INSTALL IS NOT A WORKSPACE, AND A WATCHDOG WITH NO
# WORKSPACE MUST NOT INVENT ONE. D27's definition — implemented canonically by
# `ignite/ignite-cli/lib/config.js#findInstallRoot` ("walk up to the NEAREST ancestor holding
# `.rbtv/modules/ignite/server.json`") — is the folder that roots the INSTALL RECORD, never any
# folder that happens to contain a `.rbtv/` directory. The rule is MIRRORED here in five lines
# rather than imported: this tool is pure stdlib on purpose (it must still run when everything
# else is down), and the only Python port of config.js — `ignite/coord/gateway_client.py` — takes
# the workspace root as an ARGUMENT and owns no walker to reuse.
#
# ⚠ WHY THE OLD `os.getcwd()` DEFAULT WAS A DEFECT AND NOT A CONVENIENCE (wave test 15,
# 2026-08-28). Every path this tool writes is `<WORKSPACE>/.rbtv/runtime/…`, so a pass with no env
# and a cwd that is not a workspace CREATES `<cwd>/.rbtv/` on its first write. At
# 2026-08-28T03:02:15Z `probes/probe-watchdog-staged-failure` spawned this tool from the rbtv REPO
# root with no `RBTV_WATCHDOG_WORKSPACE`, and planted `<repo>/.rbtv/runtime/watchdog/`. That folder
# then OUT-VOTED the real install for the other walker on this box —
# `ignite/deploy/probe-suite-scheduled.py#find_workspace_root`, which stopped at the first ancestor
# holding a bare `.rbtv/` — so from the 04:00Z fire the probe suite wrote its `latest.json` into
# the repo while THIS tool (unit env pointing at the vault) kept reading the vault copy, frozen at
# 03:20:11Z. Result: 195 consecutive passes reporting `probe-suite down — last fired 9026s ago`,
# a `rbtv-probe-suite.timer` restart every minute and an owner DM — all false, for a suite that
# fired at 04:00, 05:00, 06:00 and 07:00. A stray `.rbtv/` is gitignored (`**/.rbtv/`), so nothing
# in `git status` ever showed it.
#
# ⇒ So: the env override wins outright and is NOT second-guessed (the unit sets it, and every probe
# points it at a scratch workspace that deliberately carries no install record). The cwd is
# accepted ONLY when it roots the install. Otherwise `WORKSPACE` is None and `main()` REFUSES with
# exit 2 — never a guess, because a watchdog aimed at a directory nothing writes to reports a
# healthy system as dead and a dead one as healthy with equal confidence, which is exactly the
# absence-reads-as-health failure this component exists to remove.
INSTALL_RECORD_REL = os.path.join(".rbtv", "modules", "ignite", "server.json")


def roots_install(d):
    return os.path.isfile(os.path.join(d, INSTALL_RECORD_REL))


def resolve_workspace():
    explicit = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_WORKSPACE")
    if explicit:
        return explicit
    cwd = os.getcwd()
    return cwd if roots_install(cwd) else None


WORKSPACE = resolve_workspace()
GATEWAY = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_GATEWAY", "http://127.0.0.1:7431/")
DAEMON_UNIT = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_DAEMON_UNIT", "rbtv-ignite.service")
BRIDGE_UNIT = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_BRIDGE_UNIT", "rbtv-chat-bridge.service")
PROBE_TIMER = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_PROBE_TIMER", "rbtv-probe-suite.timer")
TIMEOUT = float(env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_SECONDS", "10"))


def operator_path():
    p = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_OPERATOR")
    if p:
        return p
    # The operator verb is a sibling COMPONENT, not a sibling capability: the
    # component-first move put the watchdog under `observation/` and left
    # `daemon-operator/` under `operator/`, so this walks to the module root.
    ignite_root = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(
        os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))))
    sibling = os.path.join(ignite_root, "operator", "daemon-operator", "tool",
                           "rbtv-ignite-daemon")
    return sibling if os.path.exists(sibling) else "rbtv-ignite-daemon"


def utc_epoch(stamp):
    """Both artifacts this reads stamp UTC with a literal Z. calendar.timegm is the
    UTC-correct inverse; mktime()+timezone silently skews by an hour under DST on a
    box that is not UTC, and this component must be portable off this one host."""
    return calendar.timegm(time.strptime(stamp, "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"))


def run(argv, env_extra=None):
    e = dict(os.environ)
    e.update(env_extra or {})
    p = subprocess.run(argv, capture_output=True, text=True, env=e, timeout=180)
    return p.returncode, (p.stdout + p.stderr).strip()


def gateway_call(target):
    """Returns (state, detail). state: 'ok' | 'down' | 'auth'."""
    # No fallback to IGNITE_SENDER_TOKEN: borrowing another sender's token would make
    # every probe land in the audit columns under the wrong identity AND would silently
    # satisfy the mint the enabling gate exists to force (D5 — the watchdog presents a
    # sender identity minted the sanctioned way). Missing = say so, loudly.
    token = env("IGNITE_WATCHDOG_TOKEN")
    if not token:
        return "auth", "no IGNITE_WATCHDOG_TOKEN in the environment — the watchdog's own gateway sender is not minted or not loaded"
    body = json.dumps({"intent": "inspect", "payload": {"target": target}}).encode()
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        GATEWAY, data=body,
        headers=dict(UA, authorization="Bearer " + token))
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=TIMEOUT) as r:
            return "ok", json.loads(r.read())
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as ex:
        # 401 with a known-good token means the daemon is UP and something else is
        # wrong. Restarting would not fix it and would loop; report instead.
        if ex.code == 401:
            return "auth", "gateway answered 401 AUTH_REFUSED — the daemon is up, the token is not accepted"
        return "down", "gateway HTTP %s" % ex.code
    except Exception as ex:  # connection refused, timeout, DNS, malformed body
        return "down", "%s: %s" % (type(ex).__name__, ex)


# ── the four probes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each returns (state, detail): 'up' · 'down' · 'alarm' (up, but a human is needed
# and no restart can help) · 'skip' (nothing for this row to grade — either another
# row already owns the cause, or the subject is not scheduled at all).

def probe_daemon():
    state, detail = gateway_call("daemon")
    if state == "ok":
        r = detail.get("result", {})
        return "up", "pid=%s uptime_ms=%s last_tick=%s" % (
            r.get("pid"), r.get("uptime_ms"), r.get("last_tick"))
    if state == "auth":
        return "alarm", detail
    return "down", detail


def probe_bridge():
    rc, out = run(["systemctl", "--user", "is-active", BRIDGE_UNIT])
    active = out.strip()
    if active != "active":
        return "down", "is-active=%s" % (active or "<unreadable>")
    # `active` only proves the Node process exists. Slack's socket can die under it;
    # the bridge's own reconnect() is the first line of self-heal, so what this
    # catches is that BACKOFF LOOP being stuck: the newest socket-lifecycle line in
    # the journal is a reconnect failure with no later hello. Neither marker present
    # is NOT a fault — a healthy bridge is quiet.
    _, log = run(["journalctl", "--user", "-u", BRIDGE_UNIT, "-n", "200",
                  "--no-pager", "-o", "cat"])
    last = None
    for line in log.splitlines():
        if HELLO_MARK in line:
            last = "hello"
        elif RECONNECT_FAIL_MARK in line:
            last = "reconnect-failed"
    if last == "reconnect-failed":
        return "down", "active, but its newest Socket Mode line is a reconnect failure with no later hello"
    return "up", "active, socket marker=%s" % (last or "none-in-window")


def probe_probe_suite():
    path = os.path.join(WORKSPACE, ".rbtv", "runtime", "probe-suite", "latest.json")
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            a = json.load(f)
    except Exception as ex:
        return "down", "cannot read %s (%s)" % (path, type(ex).__name__)
    # LIVENESS FIRST, then CORRECTNESS. This row historically read `verdict`/`passed`/`failed`
    # NEVER — liveness only. Owner ruling `d-probe-suite-verdict-delivery` (2026-08-10) REVERSES
    # that: the runner now classifies exit-2 INOPERATIVE apart from `failed`, so the verdict is
    # meaningful (not permanently RED on by-design refusals), and this — the one consumer that
    # already opens latest.json every fire and already owns an operator delivery path — reads it.
    # A failing probe is not a liveness problem and no restart fixes it, so a live RED is an
    # `alarm` (human needed), never a `down` (which would restart the timer). Owner ruling
    # 2026-08-11 WIDENS that from RED alone to ANY verdict that is not GREEN/UNKNOWN: the
    # runner also grades ITSELF broken (ERROR · COVERAGE-MISMATCH · ARTIFACT-PATH-MISMATCH ·
    # ARTIFACT-MISSING · INCOMPLETE, and whatever it learns to say next), and a run that
    # produced no clean grade is exactly as un-restartable and as human-needing as a RED one.
    # GREEN, UNKNOWN and a missing/empty verdict stay `up` — the liveness branches above are
    # untouched, so "the suite is dead" and "the suite is broken" never collapse into one word.
    fired = a.get("fired_at")
    stale_after = a.get("stale_after_seconds")
    if not fired or not stale_after:
        return "down", "artifact carries no fired_at/stale_after_seconds"
    age = time.time() - utc_epoch(fired)
    if age > float(stale_after):
        return "down", "last fired %ds ago, stale_after=%ss" % (int(age), stale_after)
    live = "last fired %ds ago (stale_after=%ss)" % (int(age), stale_after)
    verdict = a.get("verdict")
    if isinstance(verdict, str) and verdict not in ("", "GREEN", "UNKNOWN"):
        if verdict == "RED":
            failed = a.get("failed")
            return "alarm", ("suite is LIVE but the correctness verdict is RED: %s genuine probe "
                             "failure(s) (INOPERATIVE/by-design refusals already excluded) — %s"
                             % (failed if failed is not None else "?", live))
        # The runner-grade-broken verdicts carry no `failed` count — they carry a `note` (or an
        # `error` on the ERROR path). Read whichever is there; never assume either.
        why = a.get("note") or a.get("error")
        return "alarm", ("suite is LIVE but produced no clean grade: verdict=%s%s — %s"
                         % (verdict, (" — %s" % why) if why else "", live))
    return "up", "%s, verdict=%s" % (live, verdict or "?")


# ── daemon identity · restart · crash-loop · stale code ─────────────────────
# Ported from `ignite/coord/watch.py` (`daemon_identity`, `_daemon_change`,
# `daemon_code_state`, `check_daemon`) when that file was deleted — task 7.35, run
# decision D8. WHY THEY COULD NOT JUST RIDE THE `daemon` ROW ABOVE: that row asks the
# GATEWAY, and the gateway's answer carries pid/uptime/last_tick and NO identity that can
# be correlated across passes. These four verdicts are therefore read from systemd for the
# daemon unit and compared against the PREVIOUS pass's reading on disk.
#
# They are a REPORT row, deliberately outside the restart table: not one of them is fixed
# by a restart, and the `daemon` row above already owns the only restart lever this unit
# has. CMP-28 invariant 2 holds — probe · restart · report, never interpret.


def daemon_state_path():
    return env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_DAEMON_STATE",
               os.path.join(WORKSPACE, ".rbtv", "runtime", "watchdog", "daemon.json"))


def load_daemon_state():
    try:
        with open(daemon_state_path()) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except Exception:
        return {}


def write_json_atomic(path, obj):
    """THE state-file write path of this tool — tmp beside the target, then os.replace.
    All three state files (state.json · daemon.json · failcount.json) go through it: a
    watchdog fired every 60s is killed mid-write eventually, and a torn state file is read
    back as "no prior pass", which re-arms every announcement it exists to suppress."""
    p = os.path.abspath(path)
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
    tmp = p + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(obj, f)
    os.replace(tmp, p)


def save_daemon_state(st):
    """Its OWN file, next to state.json rather than inside it: the alert-dedupe record is
    rewritten wholesale on every pass (and cleared to None when all green), so parking a
    prior-pass identity in it would erase the identity exactly when the system is healthy —
    which is every pass a restart has to be detected ACROSS."""
    write_json_atomic(daemon_state_path(), st)


# ── the persistent outage ledger [T4-R9, C-17] ──────────────────────────────
# ⚠ THE BIT-7 SURFACE. On 2026-08-19 the daemon was down 18:18:44→21:24:59 (3h05m) and the only
# record of it was `.rbtv/runtime/watchdog/daemon.json` reading `restarts: 0` — a field that counts
# systemd's OWN restart events (NRestarts) and therefore reads CLEAN through a straight outage
# nobody tried to restart. daemon.json is a SNAPSHOT: it is rewritten every pass, so it can only
# ever say what is true now, never what happened. This file is the other half — append-only, one
# JSON object per line, never rewritten, so a decision taken at 18:19 is still readable at 21:30
# and across any number of watchdog and daemon restarts.
# EVERY decision is written, not only the acted ones: a WITHHELD restart is the decision that made
# the 3h05m invisible, and a ledger that records only actions is silent in exactly the case it
# exists for.

def ledger_path():
    return env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_LEDGER",
               os.path.join(WORKSPACE, ".rbtv", "runtime", "watchdog", "outage-ledger.jsonl"))


def ledger(decision, reason, **fields):
    """Append one decision row. Returns the row (so a caller can print or test it).

    Append-mode + one line per record, deliberately: the snapshot files go through
    write_json_atomic because a torn REWRITE reads back as "no prior pass", but a ledger is only
    ever extended, and O_APPEND on one short line is the cheapest durable write there is. A
    watchdog killed mid-write loses at most the row it was writing, never the history.

    ⚠ NEVER RAISES. A ledger that can abort the pass would turn the record-keeping into a new way
    for the watchdog to go blind — the exact failure class this component exists to remove. A
    write that fails is reported on the row's own return and nowhere else."""
    row = dict(fields)
    row["at"] = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    row["decision"] = decision
    row["reason"] = reason
    try:
        p = os.path.abspath(ledger_path())
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
        with open(p, "a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(row, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    except OSError as exc:
        row["ledger_write_error"] = "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)
    return row


# ── the non-Slack dead-man [T4-R9, C-17, spec-owner-io §8] ──────────────────
# The CP1-ruled channel: a healthchecks-style HTTPS ping fired from THIS timer on every pass that
# reads the daemon determinately healthy. The signal is the ABSENCE of the ping — the check service
# alerts out-of-band (email/SMS) when one is missed. That is the only alert left standing when
# Slack is unreachable, when the outbox cannot deliver, AND when this watchdog itself is dead —
# which is the branch no in-band alarm can ever cover.
# ⚠ NO CHECK URL IS HARDCODED (repo law: per-instance inputs resolve at runtime). No URL configured
# means no dead-man, said out loud on the row, never a silent no-op.

def deadman_url():
    return env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_DEADMAN_URL")


def deadman_ping():
    """Returns (state, detail). state: 'pinged' | 'dry' | 'off' | 'failed'.

    ⚠ DRY MODE IS A HARD WALL, AND IT IS CHECKED BEFORE THE URL. RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_FILE is
    already this tool's "send nothing, write what you would have sent" sink for the owner DM; a
    dead-man that ignored it would have every test and every rehearsal pinging a real check, and a
    real check that is pinged by a test is a dead-man that can never fire."""
    sink = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_FILE")
    url = deadman_url()
    if sink:
        record = {"at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()),
                  "deadman": "would-have-pinged", "url": url or None}
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(sink)), exist_ok=True)
        with open(sink, "a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps(record, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
        return "dry", "RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_FILE set — NOT pinged; would-have-pinged written to %s" % sink
    if not url:
        return "off", ("no RBTV_WATCHDOG_DEADMAN_URL — the out-of-band channel is NOT armed, so a "
                       "watchdog that dies with the daemon reaches nobody")
    try:
        req = urllib.request.Request(url, headers={"user-agent": "rbtv-ignite-watchdog"})
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=TIMEOUT) as r:
            return "pinged", "HTTP %s" % r.status
    except Exception as exc:
        return "failed", "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)


# ── the route to the ONE alarm emitter [T4-R10, spec-owner-io §9] ───────────
# CALLER-ONLY. This watchdog composes no alarm text of its own and posts to no Slack of its own:
# it hands a finished observation to `ignite/observation/emitter.js` through the sibling
# `watchdog-alarm.js` shim, and the emitter owns the schema, the persisted signature registry and
# the dedupe. The shim exists because this tool is Python and the emitter is JavaScript; see its
# own header for why a second Python emitter was refused.

def alarm_shim_path():
    return env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_ALARM_SHIM",
               os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "watchdog-alarm.js"))


def _run_alarm_shim(payload):
    """Drive the shim once and return (ok, answer). ONE body for both acts: the shim path, the
    node resolution, the JSON round-trip and the never-raises contract are the same for an emit
    and for a clear, and two copies of them is how the two acts come to disagree about which
    registry they are talking to.

    ⚠ NEVER RAISES, for the same reason `ledger` does not: the alarm path failing must not be able
    to kill the pass that was trying to raise it."""
    shim = alarm_shim_path()
    if not os.path.exists(shim):
        return False, {"error": "alarm shim not found at %s" % shim}
    try:
        p = subprocess.run([env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_NODE", "node"), shim],
                           input=json.dumps(payload), capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
    except Exception as exc:
        return False, {"error": "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)}
    out = (p.stdout or "").strip()
    try:
        answer = json.loads(out.splitlines()[-1]) if out else {}
    except (ValueError, IndexError):
        answer = {}
    if p.returncode != 0 or not answer.get("ok"):
        return False, {"error": answer.get("error")
                       or (p.stderr or out or "exit %s" % p.returncode).strip()[:300]}
    return True, answer


def emit_alarm(condition, subject_id, evidence, clears_when, signature_class, subject_type="daemon"):
    """Emit ONE system-health alarm through the emitter. Returns (ok, detail).

    `immediate` is True and not a judgement call: spec §9.2 makes system-health alarms
    digest-exempt for their first post [CF-9, T5-R11], and a watchdog N-fail is named there as one.

    `subject_type` defaults to `daemon` so the N-fail caller reads exactly as it always did; the
    row alarms below name the row they observed instead.

    ⚠ NEVER RAISES — see `_run_alarm_shim`."""
    ok, answer = _run_alarm_shim({"condition": condition,
                                  "subject": {"type": subject_type, "id": subject_id},
                                  "evidence_pointer": evidence,
                                  "what_would_clear_it": clears_when,
                                  "signature_class": signature_class,
                                  "immediate": True,
                                  "workspace_root": WORKSPACE})
    if not ok:
        return False, answer.get("error") or "alarm shim refused with no reason"
    return True, "posted=%s reason=%s signature=%s outbox_id=%s delivered=%s" % (
        answer.get("posted"), answer.get("reason"), answer.get("signature"),
        answer.get("outbox_id"), answer.get("delivered"))


def clear_alarm(subject_type, subject_id, signature_class):
    """Close an OPEN registry row whose condition this pass observed to be over. Returns
    (ok, cleared, detail).

    THE OTHER HALF OF THE ALARM. An emitter row stays OPEN until somebody says it is not, and an
    open row nobody closes turns the standing-condition surface into a list of everything that has
    ever been wrong — which reads exactly like a system that is still wrong. Clearing is SILENT by
    the emitter's own rule: nothing is posted, the row simply stops appearing.

    ⚠ NEVER RAISES — see `_run_alarm_shim`."""
    ok, answer = _run_alarm_shim({"act": "clear",
                                  "subject": {"type": subject_type, "id": subject_id},
                                  "signature_class": signature_class,
                                  "workspace_root": WORKSPACE})
    if not ok:
        return False, False, answer.get("error") or "alarm shim refused with no reason"
    return True, bool(answer.get("cleared")), "cleared=%s signature=%s" % (
        answer.get("cleared"), answer.get("signature"))


# ── N consecutive non-healthy passes → ONE alarm + a ledger row [T4-R9] ─────
# ⚠⚠ THIS IS THE BIT-7 ROOT-CAUSE FIX, and it is a deliberate amendment of the "UNKNOWN NEVER
# pushes" calibration in check_daemon above. THE DIAGNOSED MECHANISM: check_daemon fires its DOWN
# note only on a DETERMINATE `stopped`, and `_daemon_change` refuses to claim a change when either
# side is `unknown` — so an outage the identity read could not resolve determinately produced NO
# note (no alert, no DM) and NO change record (no ledger, `restarts` frozen at 0). The proof is in
# the artifact the incident left: `.rbtv/runtime/watchdog/daemon.json`, read at 21:30 on
# 2026-08-19 — six passes after the daemon came back at 21:23:39 on a FRESH invocation id — still
# carried NO `notified_daemon_invocation` key, which is only reachable when `_daemon_change`
# returned None, which requires the previously persisted reading to have been non-determinate.
# `unknown` was a silent state, and silence is what a liveness component may never produce.
#
# WHAT IS KEPT FROM THE OLD CALIBRATION: `unknown` still never claims a DOWN. The alarm below says
# what was actually observed — "not determinately healthy for N consecutive passes, last reading
# <state>: <why>" — so a measurement that failed is still reported as a measurement that failed,
# never dressed up as an outage. What changes is that it is reported AT ALL.
#
# N is `STRIKES`, the watchdog's existing consecutive-fail threshold (spec-owner-io §8: N stays the
# existing threshold). The alarm fires ONCE PER EPISODE and re-arms on recovery: §9.2 gives one
# alarm per condition-signature and hands re-surfacing to the 2-hourly system digest, so a second
# emission would be the volume violation, not the coverage the episode needs.

def daemon_health_streak(st, ident, dry=False):
    """Count CONSECUTIVE non-determinately-healthy daemon readings, ledger every one, alarm at N,
    and ping the dead-man on health. Mutates `st` (the persisted daemon-state record). Returns the
    report row."""
    state = (ident or {}).get("state")
    why = (ident or {}).get("why") or ""
    streak = int(st.get("unhealthy_streak") or 0)

    if state == "running":
        note = "healthy"
        if streak:
            started = float(st.get("unhealthy_since") or time.time())
            seconds = int(time.time() - started)
            # THE FIELD TASK #113 ASKED FOR: the outage DURATION, recorded as a first-class value
            # rather than left derivable by hand from two `since` stamps a reader must think to
            # diff. `restarts` stays what it is — systemd's own event counter — because the fix for
            # "that counter cannot see a straight outage" is another field, never a redefined one.
            st["last_outage_seconds"] = seconds
            st["last_outage_ended_at"] = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
            st["last_outage_passes"] = streak
            if not dry:
                ledger("recovered", "the daemon unit reads determinately running again",
                       unit=DAEMON_UNIT, unhealthy_passes=streak, outage_seconds=seconds,
                       invocation=(ident or {}).get("invocation"))
            note = "recovered after %d non-healthy pass(es) — outage %ds, recorded" % (streak, seconds)
        st["unhealthy_streak"] = 0
        st.pop("unhealthy_since", None)
        # Re-arm: a SECOND episode must alarm. Only a determinate `running` clears it, exactly as
        # the down-flag next door — an `unknown` pass clears nothing.
        st.pop("alarmed_unhealthy_at", None)
        # ⚠ A --dry-run PINGS NOTHING. The dead-man's whole value is that a MISSED ping is the
        # alert, so a diagnostic verb that pings would silence a real out-of-band alarm for the
        # length of its own period — the same reason a dry run must not DM.
        if dry:
            return "%-13s %-6s %s | dead-man: --dry-run, NOT pinged" % ("  ->", "health", note)
        dm_state, dm_detail = deadman_ping()
        return "%-13s %-6s %s | dead-man %s: %s" % ("  ->", "health", note, dm_state, dm_detail)

    streak += 1
    st["unhealthy_streak"] = streak
    if not st.get("unhealthy_since"):
        st["unhealthy_since"] = time.time()
    detail = why or "state=%s" % (state or "?")
    if not dry:
        ledger("observed-not-healthy",
               "the daemon unit did not read determinately running: %s" % detail,
               unit=DAEMON_UNIT, identity_state=state, consecutive_passes=streak, threshold=STRIKES)
    if streak < STRIKES or dry:
        return "%-13s %-6s NOT healthy — %s (consecutive pass %d of %d before alarm)" % (
            "  ->", "health", detail, streak, STRIKES)
    if st.get("alarmed_unhealthy_at"):
        return "%-13s %-6s NOT healthy — %s (consecutive pass %d; already alarmed at %s)" % (
            "  ->", "health", detail, streak, st["alarmed_unhealthy_at"])
    held = int(time.time() - float(st.get("unhealthy_since") or time.time()))
    ok, note = emit_alarm(
        condition=("The ignite daemon has not read determinately healthy for %d consecutive "
                   "watchdog passes (about %d minutes). The last reading of unit %s was `%s`: %s. "
                   "Nothing is running jobs, ticks or spawns while this holds, and a restart may "
                   "have been withheld — the outage ledger says which."
                   % (streak, held // 60, DAEMON_UNIT, state or "unreadable", detail)),
        subject_id=DAEMON_UNIT,
        evidence=ledger_path(),
        clears_when="the watchdog reads the unit determinately running again on any pass",
        signature_class="watchdog-daemon-unhealthy")
    if ok:
        st["alarmed_unhealthy_at"] = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
        ledger("alarmed", "N consecutive non-healthy passes reached the threshold",
               unit=DAEMON_UNIT, consecutive_passes=streak, threshold=STRIKES, emitter=note)
    else:
        # An alarm that could not be emitted is itself a fact worth keeping, and NOT stamping
        # `alarmed_unhealthy_at` is what makes the next pass try again.
        ledger("alarm-emit-failed", note, unit=DAEMON_UNIT, consecutive_passes=streak)
    return "%-13s %-6s NOT healthy — %s (consecutive pass %d of %d) | alarm ok=%s %s" % (
        "  ->", "health", detail, streak, STRIKES, ok, note)


def _int_or_none(kv, key):
    """systemd's counter as an int, or None when absent/unparseable — NEVER 0 on failure.
    0 is a MEANINGFUL value here (a unit that has never restarted), so defaulting a failed
    read to 0 reports the healthiest possible answer at the moment the read broke. None is
    the only honest absence, and the caller reads it as "no comparison possible"."""
    try:
        return int(kv.get(key))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None


def daemon_identity():
    """The daemon unit's systemd identity THIS PASS — or UNKNOWN, said out loud.

    ⚠⚠ `--user` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. The unit is user-scoped. Asking
    the SYSTEM bus for it returns, measured on this box against the live daemon:
        LoadState=not-found  ActiveState=inactive  SubState=dead  MainPID=0  exit 0
    which is BYTE-IDENTICAL to what the user bus returns for a unit that genuinely does not
    exist — exit 0 in both cases. So "the daemon is gone" and "I asked the wrong bus" are
    THE SAME ANSWER. ⇒ `not-found` IS REPORTED AS UNKNOWN, NEVER AS ABSENT. A determinate
    `loaded` + `inactive` IS reportable as stopped: there the unit answered for itself.

    Returns one of:
        {"state": "running", "unit", "pid", "since", "invocation", "restarts"}
        {"state": "stopped", "unit", "why", "restarts"}   — the unit answered; it is down
        {"state": "unknown", "unit", "why"}               — the question could not be answered
    `invocation` is systemd's InvocationID, minted fresh on every start: the restart
    comparison keys on it rather than MainPID, which the kernel recycles and which carries
    no promise of uniqueness across time."""
    props = ["LoadState", "ActiveState", "SubState", "MainPID", "ActiveEnterTimestamp",
             "InvocationID", "NRestarts"]
    try:
        rc, out = run(["systemctl", "--user", "show", DAEMON_UNIT,
                       "--property=" + ",".join(props)])
    except Exception as exc:
        return {"state": "unknown", "unit": DAEMON_UNIT,
                "why": "systemctl --user did not answer (%s)" % exc}
    if rc != 0:
        return {"state": "unknown", "unit": DAEMON_UNIT,
                "why": "systemctl --user exited %s: %s"
                       % (rc, (out or "").strip()[:120] or "no output")}
    kv = {}
    for line in (out or "").splitlines():
        if "=" in line:
            k, _, v = line.partition("=")
            kv[k.strip()] = v.strip()
    load = kv.get("LoadState")
    if not load:
        return {"state": "unknown", "unit": DAEMON_UNIT,
                "why": "systemctl --user printed no LoadState"}
    if load != "loaded":
        return {"state": "unknown", "unit": DAEMON_UNIT,
                "why": "LoadState=%s — indistinguishable from asking the wrong bus, so this is "
                       "NOT reported as absent" % load}
    active = kv.get("ActiveState", "")
    if active != "active":
        return {"state": "stopped", "unit": DAEMON_UNIT,
                "restarts": _int_or_none(kv, "NRestarts"),
                "why": "the unit answered for itself: ActiveState=%s SubState=%s"
                       % (active or "?", kv.get("SubState") or "?")}
    invocation = kv.get("InvocationID") or ""
    pid = kv.get("MainPID") or ""
    if not invocation or not pid.isdigit() or int(pid) <= 0:
        return {"state": "unknown", "unit": DAEMON_UNIT,
                "why": "unit is active but its identity is unreadable "
                       "(MainPID=%s, InvocationID=%s)"
                       % (pid or "?", "set" if invocation else "empty")}
    return {"state": "running", "unit": DAEMON_UNIT, "pid": int(pid),
            "restarts": _int_or_none(kv, "NRestarts"),
            "since": kv.get("ActiveEnterTimestamp") or "", "invocation": invocation}


def _daemon_change(prev, now):
    """The restart record to persist, or None — given the PREVIOUS pass's reading and this
    one's. A change is claimed ONLY between determinate readings: a transition into or out
    of `unknown` is a MEASUREMENT failing, not an event observed, and reporting it as a
    restart would manufacture exactly the false certainty this exists to remove."""
    if not isinstance(prev, dict) or not isinstance(now, dict):
        return None
    if prev.get("state") == "unknown" or now.get("state") == "unknown":
        return None
    if (prev.get("state") == now.get("state")
            and prev.get("invocation", "") == now.get("invocation", "")):
        return None
    return {"at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()), "from": prev, "to": now}


def identity_verdict(prev, now):
    """IDENTITY — the running daemon COMPARED against the one previously seen, never merely
    printed. The gateway row prints a pid and compares it to nothing, which is how a
    swapped-out process reads as continuous health. Both keys are compared: the
    InvocationID because it is the only per-start unique, the MainPID because a pid that
    moved under an UNCHANGED invocation is an anomaly worth saying out loud rather than
    silently folding into "same"."""
    if not isinstance(now, dict) or now.get("state") != "running":
        return "no comparison — this pass has no determinate identity (%s)" % (
            (now or {}).get("state") or "?")
    if not isinstance(prev, dict) or prev.get("state") != "running":
        return "no comparison — no determinate previous reading (first pass, or the last one " \
               "was %s)" % ((prev or {}).get("state") or "absent")
    same_inv = prev.get("invocation") == now.get("invocation")
    same_pid = prev.get("pid") == now.get("pid")
    if same_inv and same_pid:
        return "SAME as the previous pass (pid %s, invocation %s)" % (
            now.get("pid"), str(now.get("invocation"))[:12])
    return "CHANGED — pid %s -> %s, invocation %s -> %s" % (
        prev.get("pid"), now.get("pid"),
        str(prev.get("invocation"))[:12], str(now.get("invocation"))[:12])


def code_scope_note(root, entries):
    """The verdict's OWN SCOPE, said in the verdict (closeout task 23, 2026-08-17). WHY THE
    SCOPE-STATEMENT ROUTE AND NOT A WIDER FINGERPRINT: the marker covers only what sat in the
    DAEMON's require.cache under the marker's root at boot. That root is the daemon's own
    component tree (`ignite/runtime/`, and `ignite/server/` on a marker written before the
    component-first move), so EVERY sibling component the daemon loads from is outside it —
    `supervisor/`, `planning/`, `envelope/`, `operator/`, `observation/` and `state-store/` all
    sat inside the old root as `engine/**` and no longer do — and `chat/**` runs in a DIFFERENT
    process (the chat bridge unit), so its modules can never appear in this daemon's
    require.cache at all. Widening the boot capture is daemon JS (inert until a restart this
    program does not have) and still could not reach the bridge — so `current` names what it
    covers instead, because a bare `current` was read as whole-tree-current and hid a
    landed-but-inert file for ~15h (the W8 failure). Computed per pass from the marker itself,
    never hardcoded: a future boot that widens the root makes the uncovered list shrink on its
    own instead of printing a stale disclaimer."""
    base = root.parent if root.name in ("runtime", "server") else root
    blind = []
    for name in ("supervisor", "planning", "envelope", "operator", "observation",
                 "state-store", "chat"):
        tree = base / name
        try:
            if tree.is_dir() and not any(
                    Path(os.path.abspath(str(root / rel))).is_relative_to(tree)
                    for rel in entries):
                blind.append("%s/%s/**" % (base.name, name))
        except Exception:
            continue
    if not blind:
        return ""
    return (" — SCOPE: only the %d files fingerprinted at boot under %s; %s NOT covered, drift "
            "there is INVISIBLE to this check (use the per-file mtime-vs-unit-start check)"
            % (len(entries), root, ", ".join(blind)))


def daemon_code_state(workspace_root, daemon):
    """Is the DAEMON running current code? Returns (verdict, detail).

    ⚠⚠ THE MARKER OUTLIVES THE PROCESS THAT WROTE IT, and that is the whole reason this is
    careful. It is a file: if the daemon dies, `.rbtv/runtime/daemon-code.json` SURVIVES
    carrying the last boot's fingerprint. A reader trusting it standalone would report "code
    is current" about a daemon that is not running — absence-reading-as-health, arriving
    through the artifact built to prevent it.

    ⇒ THREE OUTCOMES, KEPT DISTINCT; COLLAPSING ANY TWO IS THE DEFECT:
        "stale"    the marker MATCHES the live unit's boot AND named files changed on disk.
        "current"  the marker matches the live unit's boot and every named file agrees.
        "unknown"  anything else — no marker, corrupt marker, a marker from a DIFFERENT
                   boot, or a daemon that is not determinately running. NEVER stale, NEVER
                   current.
    ⚠ IDENTITY FIRST, BYTES SECOND. A byte comparison against a marker from a previous boot
    is arithmetic about a process that no longer exists — and hash-first would return a
    false `current` across a restart-without-redeploy, which is the common case."""
    if not isinstance(daemon, dict) or daemon.get("state") != "running":
        return "unknown", "the daemon is not determinately running, so nothing can be correlated"
    p = Path(workspace_root) / ".rbtv" / "runtime" / "daemon-code.json"
    try:
        marker = json.loads(p.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return "unknown", ("no boot marker at .rbtv/runtime/daemon-code.json — this daemon "
                           "booted before the marker existed, or its write failed")
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, OSError) as exc:
        return "unknown", "the boot marker is unreadable (%s)" % exc.__class__.__name__
    if not isinstance(marker, dict):
        return "unknown", "the boot marker is not an object"
    live_inv, mark_inv = daemon.get("invocation"), marker.get("invocation")
    if not mark_inv or not live_inv or mark_inv != live_inv:
        return "unknown", ("the marker is from a DIFFERENT boot than the running unit "
                           "(marker %s, live %s), so it describes a process that is gone"
                           % (str(mark_inv)[:12] or "none", str(live_inv)[:12]))
    entries = (marker.get("code") or {}).get("entries")
    if not isinstance(entries, dict) or not entries:
        # Empty-but-present is the state that lies; the writer never emits it, and a reader
        # that accepted it would report "nothing drifted" forever.
        return "unknown", "the marker carries no file fingerprints"
    # ⚠ THE ROOT IS TAKEN FROM THE MARKER, NEVER RE-DERIVED HERE. The writer knows its own
    # root and says so; a literal here would freeze one install's layout into a component
    # every install runs.
    root = marker.get("code_root") or marker.get("root")
    if not root:
        return "unknown", ("the marker does not say which root its paths are relative to, so "
                           "they cannot be resolved without guessing this install's layout")
    root = Path(root)
    drifted = []
    for rel, want in sorted(entries.items()):
        try:
            got = hashlib.sha256((root / rel).read_bytes()).hexdigest()
        except OSError:
            drifted.append("%s (unreadable now)" % rel)
            continue
        if got != want:
            drifted.append(rel)
    if drifted:
        return "stale", ", ".join(drifted[:6]) + (
            " (+%d more)" % (len(drifted) - 6) if len(drifted) > 6 else "")
    # `stale` needs no scope suffix: it is a positive signal. `current` is the verdict that was
    # misread as whole-tree-current, so it carries its scope — see code_scope_note above.
    return "current", "%d files match the bytes this daemon booted on%s" % (
        len(entries), code_scope_note(root, entries))


def check_daemon(sysstate, daemon, change, notes, code=None):
    """The four verdicts as AT MOST ONE alert per pass. Mutates `sysstate` (the prior-pass
    record) and appends to `notes`; returns the report row.

    ⚠ THE CALIBRATION. A note earns its interruption by naming a HARD, MEASURABLE
    transition — never a judgment, never the watchdog's own difficulty:
      DOWN       once per EPISODE, re-arming when the unit returns.
      RESTARTED  once per EVENT, keyed on the NEW InvocationID — never on a change record
                 merely existing, which would re-announce the same restart forever.
      CRASH-LOOP re-fires while `NRestarts` CLIMBS. DOWN is binary and gets one note, but a
                 crash loop is MONOTONIC (2026-07-27: NRestarts=32 and climbing every 5s,
                 learned by hand) — deterioration, not repetition. Read from systemd's OWN
                 counter, never counted from this pass's sampling: a 60s watchdog counting
                 a 5s loop would undercount by two orders of magnitude.
      STALE CODE once per deploy, keyed on invocation + drift set.
      UNKNOWN    NEVER pushes, in any of the four. It means THE MEASUREMENT FAILED, not that
                 something happened; a flag that fires on the watchdog's own blindness is
                 what trains a reader to discount the next one. It stays LOUD on the row.
    ⚠ The alert TEXTS carry their dedupe key BEFORE the em-dash (`[restarts N]`, `[inv X]`)
    because the pass-level dedupe fingerprints on the head of each alert: without the key a
    climbing crash loop and a second restart would both read as an unchanged repeat and be
    suppressed for the whole re-alert window."""
    state = (daemon or {}).get("state")
    restarts = (daemon or {}).get("restarts")
    seen = sysstate.get("daemon_restarts_flagged")
    climbing = isinstance(restarts, int) and isinstance(seen, int) and restarts > seen
    lines = []
    if state == "stopped":
        if not sysstate.get("notified_daemon_down") or climbing:
            loop = (" ⚠ AND IT IS CRASH-LOOPING: systemd's own restart count has risen "
                    "%s -> %s since the last warning, so it is failing repeatedly rather than "
                    "sitting still." % (seen, restarts) if climbing else
                    " Announced ONCE for this outage.")
            lines.append("daemon: IGNITE DAEMON IS DOWN [restarts %s] — %s. Nothing is running "
                         "jobs, ticks or spawns.%s"
                         % (restarts if isinstance(restarts, int) else "?",
                            (daemon or {}).get("why") or "unit inactive", loop))
            sysstate["notified_daemon_down"] = True
            if isinstance(restarts, int):
                sysstate["daemon_restarts_flagged"] = restarts
    elif state == "running":
        # Re-arm on recovery so a SECOND outage is announced. Only a determinate `running`
        # clears it — the `unknown` fall-through below deliberately clears nothing.
        sysstate.pop("notified_daemon_down", None)
        to = (change or {}).get("to") or {}
        inv = to.get("invocation")
        if inv and sysstate.get("notified_daemon_invocation") != inv:
            frm = (change or {}).get("from") or {}
            lines.append(
                "daemon: IGNITE DAEMON RESTARTED [inv %s] — pid %s -> %s, observed %s. An "
                "owner-side deploy or bounce is otherwise INVISIBLE; that happened twice on "
                "2026-07-27 and was noticed hours late both times. This says THAT it "
                "restarted, never WHICH CODE it loaded."
                % (str(inv)[:12], frm.get("pid") or "?", to.get("pid") or "?",
                   (change or {}).get("at") or "?"))
            sysstate["notified_daemon_invocation"] = inv
        # SEEDED QUIETLY on the running path, never flagged from here: a unit that
        # crash-looped BEFORE this code landed carries a high NRestarts, and announcing it
        # now would report an outage already lived through. A fix whose first act is a false
        # alarm teaches its reader to discount the next one.
        if isinstance(restarts, int):
            sysstate["daemon_restarts_flagged"] = restarts
    if code and code[0] == "stale":
        seen_stale = sysstate.get("notified_daemon_stale")
        key = "%s:%s" % ((daemon or {}).get("invocation"), code[1])
        if seen_stale != key:
            lines.append("daemon: IGNITE DAEMON IS RUNNING STALE CODE [%s] — %s changed on disk "
                         "since this daemon booted, and node binds a module's source at require, "
                         "so the running daemon can never pick it up. It will keep serving the "
                         "OLD behaviour while every surface reports healthy. A restart deploys "
                         "it (owner-only, task 7.68)." % (str(code[1])[:40], code[1]))
            sysstate["notified_daemon_stale"] = key
    elif code and code[0] == "current":
        sysstate.pop("notified_daemon_stale", None)
    # `unknown` falls through: no note, and NOTHING IS CLEARED. Popping the down-flag on an
    # unreadable pass would silently re-arm a duplicate announcement for an outage already
    # reported — a measurement failing must never be able to manufacture a flag.
    if lines:
        notes.append(" ".join(lines))
    label = {"running": "ok", "stopped": "DOWN", "unknown": "UNKNOWN"}.get(state, "UNKNOWN")
    detail = ("pid %s" % daemon.get("pid") if state == "running"
              else (daemon or {}).get("why") or "unreadable")
    if isinstance(restarts, int) and restarts:
        detail += " (systemd restarts: %s)" % restarts
    if code:
        # Printed in EVERY state, including current: a healthy case printing nothing leaves
        # "checked and current" to be inferred from an absence, in the one feature whose
        # whole subject is that absence and health look identical.
        # `current` carries code[1] so the scope statement reaches the report row — a bare
        # "running current code" is exactly the whole-tree misreading task 23 closed.
        detail += {"current": ", running current code (%s)" % code[1],
                   "stale": ", RUNNING STALE CODE",
                   "unknown": ", code UNKNOWN (%s)" % code[1]}.get(code[0], "")
    return "%-13s %-6s %s" % ("daemon-id", label, detail)


def daemon_verdicts(persist=True):
    """ONE reading per pass, feeding BOTH the report rows and the alerts. Returns
    (rows, alerts).

    ⚠ ONE READING IS A CORRECTNESS POINT, not tidiness: two `systemctl` calls in one pass
    can straddle a restart, and the row would then report an identity the alert contradicts.
    Two readings of one moment that disagree is the defect class this exists to close."""
    st = load_daemon_state()
    prev = st.get("daemon")
    now = daemon_identity()
    change = _daemon_change(prev, now)
    code = daemon_code_state(WORKSPACE, now)
    notes = []
    rows = [check_daemon(st, now, change, notes, code),
            "%-13s %-6s %s" % ("  ->", "ident", identity_verdict(prev, now)),
            daemon_health_streak(st, now, dry=not persist)]
    st["daemon"] = now
    if persist:
        save_daemon_state(st)
    return rows, notes


# ── restart actions ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EVERY systemd-backed row goes through the daemon-operator, which already verifies
# survival rather than taking a single read (PRIN-11 — no second restart-and-verify
# implementation). The probe-suite `.timer` was the one bypass, because the wrapper's
# survival check required a non-zero unchanged MainPID and a Timer unit's is always 0;
# commit 7127713 judges non-service units on ActiveState instead, so the bypass is gone
# rather than kept as dead flexibility. Re-measured on a throwaway timer 2026-08-08
# (review pass): wrapper exit 0, unit active after.

def restart_via_operator(unit):
    return run([operator_path(), "restart"], {"RBTV_IGNITE_UNIT": unit})


# ── the daemon row's restart gate ───────────────────────────────────────────
# ⚠⚠ THIS ROW INTERPRETS BEFORE IT ACTS, and that is a DELIBERATE amendment of the
# "probe · restart · report, never interpret" framing above (owner ruling 2026-08-14, the
# daemon-issues §2 ratification). WHY: `probe_daemon()` grades an unanswered 10s gateway call
# as `down`, but the daemon parks its event loop in execFileSync under load, so an unanswered
# call is an expected condition of a BUSY daemon and not evidence of a dead one. On
# 2026-08-14 that mapping fired 57 `restart rc=0` passes against a daemon that was alive the
# whole time, and each restart reaped the shared tmux server with every pane in it.
# The disproving datum was already in this file — `daemon_identity()` — one step DOWNSTREAM
# of the action. This moves it upstream of the action, where it can withhold one.
# ⚠ The restart lever is NOT removed and is NOT behind a flag: a genuinely dead unit
# (systemd answers `stopped`) still gets restarted once the strikes are spent. A gate that
# swallowed that case would leave the box unmonitored in the one case monitoring exists for.

STRIKES = int(env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_STRIKES", "3"))
RESTART_BACKOFF = float(env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_RESTART_BACKOFF_SECONDS", "600"))


def failcount_path():
    return env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_FAILCOUNT",
               os.path.join(WORKSPACE, ".rbtv", "runtime", "watchdog", "failcount.json"))


def load_failcount():
    try:
        with open(failcount_path()) as f:
            fc = json.load(f)
        return fc if isinstance(fc, dict) else {}
    except Exception:
        return {}


def clear_failcount():
    """Called on the daemon row's `up` verdict — the strikes are CONSECUTIVE, so one good
    pass ends the streak. Leaves `last_restart_at` alone: the backoff is about how recently
    the remedy was applied, and one healthy pass does not un-apply it."""
    fc = load_failcount()
    if fc.get("strikes"):
        fc["strikes"] = 0
        write_json_atomic(failcount_path(), fc)


def daemon_restart_gate(detail):
    """May the daemon row restart on THIS pass's `down` verdict? Returns (allow, note).

    Persists the strike count, so it counts PASSES (60s apart) and not this process's
    lifetime — a per-invocation tool has no memory of its own.

    ⚠ EVERY ARM WRITES A LEDGER ROW [T4-R9]. The three withheld arms are the ones that matter:
    a restart NOT taken is a decision, it is the decision that made the 2026-08-19 3h05m outage
    invisible, and `failcount.json` is a snapshot that only ever says what is true this pass."""
    fc = load_failcount()
    strikes = int(fc.get("strikes") or 0) + 1
    fc["strikes"] = strikes
    fc["at"] = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())
    ident = daemon_identity()
    if ident.get("state") == "running":
        # NOT a strike toward anything: systemd says the process is there. Alarm, never act.
        write_json_atomic(failcount_path(), fc)
        ledger("restart-withheld", "systemd reports the unit active, so this is a busy or wedged "
               "daemon and not a dead one", unit=DAEMON_UNIT, arm="unit-alive",
               pid=ident.get("pid"), strikes=strikes, gateway=detail)
        return False, ("daemon: THE GATEWAY IS UNANSWERABLE BUT THE DAEMON IS ALIVE — systemd "
                       "reports pid %s active since %s, so this is a busy or wedged daemon, not "
                       "a dead one, and a restart would reap every tmux pane on the box for "
                       "nothing. NOT restarting; a human is needed. Gateway said: %s"
                       % (ident.get("pid"), ident.get("since") or "?", detail))
    last = float(fc.get("last_restart_at") or 0)
    since_restart = time.time() - last
    if last and since_restart < RESTART_BACKOFF:
        write_json_atomic(failcount_path(), fc)
        ledger("restart-withheld", "the last restart was %dm ago and the verdict is still down, so "
               "re-applying the same remedy every pass is not a remedy" % (since_restart / 60),
               unit=DAEMON_UNIT, arm="backoff", backoff_seconds=RESTART_BACKOFF,
               since_last_restart_seconds=int(since_restart), strikes=strikes,
               identity_state=ident.get("state"), gateway=detail)
        return False, ("daemon: RESTART DID NOT RESTORE THE GATEWAY — backing off. The last "
                       "restart was %dm ago and the verdict is still down, so re-applying the "
                       "same remedy every pass is not a remedy. Holding for another %dm "
                       "(systemd says %s). Gateway said: %s"
                       % (since_restart / 60, (RESTART_BACKOFF - since_restart) / 60,
                          ident.get("state"), detail))
    if strikes < STRIKES:
        write_json_atomic(failcount_path(), fc)
        ledger("restart-withheld", "strike %d of %d consecutive failed passes — one missed 10s "
               "call is not an outage" % (strikes, STRIKES), unit=DAEMON_UNIT, arm="strikes",
               strikes=strikes, threshold=STRIKES, identity_state=ident.get("state"),
               gateway=detail)
        return False, ("daemon: GATEWAY DOWN, RESTART WITHHELD — strike %d of %d consecutive "
                       "failed passes (systemd says %s: %s). One missed 10s call is not an "
                       "outage. Gateway said: %s"
                       % (strikes, STRIKES, ident.get("state"),
                          ident.get("why") or ident.get("pid") or "?", detail))
    fc["strikes"] = 0
    fc["last_restart_at"] = time.time()
    write_json_atomic(failcount_path(), fc)
    ledger("restart-allowed", "%d consecutive failed passes spent the strikes and systemd does not "
           "report the unit active" % STRIKES, unit=DAEMON_UNIT, arm="strikes-spent",
           threshold=STRIKES, identity_state=ident.get("state"), gateway=detail)
    return True, None


ROWS = {
    "daemon":      (probe_daemon,       lambda: restart_via_operator(DAEMON_UNIT),
                    lambda: "restarted %s" % DAEMON_UNIT),
    "bridge":      (probe_bridge,       lambda: restart_via_operator(BRIDGE_UNIT),
                    lambda: "restarted %s" % BRIDGE_UNIT),
    "probe-suite": (probe_probe_suite,  lambda: restart_via_operator(PROBE_TIMER),
                    lambda: "restarted %s" % PROBE_TIMER),
}

# ── an `alarm` row goes to the ONE alarm registry [T4-R10, spec-owner-io §9] ─
# ⚠⚠ THE WAVE-TEST-2 ROOT-CAUSE FIX. An `alarm` verdict — "up, but a human is needed and no restart
# can help" — used to reach exactly ONE surface: the owner-DM leg below, deduped in `state.json`.
# It never reached the emitter, so `<workspace>/.rbtv/runtime/ignite/alarm-registry.json` did not
# exist on a box that had been carrying a standing probe-suite RED for hours, and EVERY reader of
# the one standing-condition surface — the §5 system digest's "Open conditions" and the daemon's
# own `inspect daemon` — was structurally blind to it. On 2026-08-26 the channel master answered a
# live owner question with "No standing warnings" while this row read `alarm` every 60 seconds.
# The DM was never the alarm surface; it was the only one that had been wired.
#
# ONE DELIVERY, NOT TWO. The row is routed through `emit_alarm` and is NOT appended to `alerts` —
# exactly how `daemon_health_streak` (the emitter's other caller here) already behaves: it returns
# a REPORT LINE and raises its alarm through the emitter, and nothing it observes enters the DM
# leg. Two deliveries of one condition is the volume violation spec §9.2 forbids, and the emitter's
# open-row signature is a durable dedupe where `state.json`'s fingerprint is a 6h re-alert timer.
#
# THE SUBJECT is the row's own subject (`{type: <row>, id: <unit>}`), so the daemon row's alarm and
# `watchdog-daemon-unhealthy` share a subject and differ only in condition class — which is what
# the emitter's signature is for. ROW_UNITS is a SEPARATE table from ROWS on purpose: ROWS is the
# probe/restart/describe triple that `main()` and the probes unpack positionally, and widening it
# to carry a fourth member would break every one of them for a fact none of them reads.

ROW_UNITS = {"daemon": DAEMON_UNIT, "bridge": BRIDGE_UNIT, "probe-suite": PROBE_TIMER}


def row_signature_class(name):
    """One condition class per row. NOT per observed text: `probe-suite` going from RED to a
    runner-grade-broken verdict is the SAME standing condition reworded, and the emitter re-posts a
    changed text on the existing row rather than minting a second one."""
    return "watchdog-%s-alarm" % name


def row_evidence(name):
    """WHERE a human opens the observation this row alarmed on — a concrete path or a journal
    query the owner can paste, never a restatement of the condition itself."""
    if name == "probe-suite":
        return os.path.join(WORKSPACE, ".rbtv", "runtime", "probe-suite", "latest.json")
    if name == "daemon":
        return "%s (gateway) · journalctl --user -u %s" % (GATEWAY, DAEMON_UNIT)
    return "journalctl --user -u %s" % ROW_UNITS.get(name, name)


def row_alarms_path():
    return env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_ROW_ALARMS",
               os.path.join(WORKSPACE, ".rbtv", "runtime", "watchdog", "row-alarms.json"))


def load_row_alarms():
    try:
        with open(row_alarms_path()) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
            return d if isinstance(d, dict) else {}
    except Exception:
        return {}


def save_row_alarms(d):
    """Its OWN file, for the same reason `daemon.json` and `failcount.json` are: `state.json` is
    the DM-dedupe record and is cleared to null on every all-green pass, which is exactly the pass
    that has to CLEAR a standing alarm — a record parked there would erase itself first."""
    write_json_atomic(row_alarms_path(), d)


def raise_row_alarm(name, detail, dry):
    """Route an `alarm` verdict into the registry. Returns the report line.

    The open-row bookkeeping mirrors `alarmed_unhealthy_at` on the N-fail path: ONE shim call per
    episode, not one per 60s pass. The emitter would dedupe a repeat anyway, but this tool is pure
    Python stdlib precisely so it still runs when everything else is down, and spawning `node`
    every minute for an answer already known is a dependency the healthy path should not carry."""
    if dry:
        return "%-13s %-6s would raise %s in the alarm registry, but --dry-run" % (
            "  ->", "alarm", row_signature_class(name))
    open_rows = load_row_alarms()
    if name in open_rows:
        return "%-13s %-6s already open in the alarm registry since %s (%s)" % (
            "  ->", "alarm", open_rows[name].get("at"), row_signature_class(name))
    ok, note = emit_alarm(
        condition=("%s: %s. No restart can fix this — a human is needed." % (name, detail)),
        subject_id=ROW_UNITS.get(name, name),
        evidence=row_evidence(name),
        clears_when="the watchdog reads the %s row `up` again on any pass" % name,
        signature_class=row_signature_class(name),
        subject_type=name)
    if ok:
        open_rows[name] = {"signature_class": row_signature_class(name),
                           "subject": ROW_UNITS.get(name, name),
                           "at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime())}
        save_row_alarms(open_rows)
        ledger("alarmed", "an `alarm` verdict was raised in the one alarm registry",
               row=name, detail=detail, signature_class=row_signature_class(name), emitter=note)
    else:
        # NOT recording the row is what makes the next pass retry — the same reason
        # `alarmed_unhealthy_at` is left unstamped on a failed emission.
        ledger("alarm-emit-failed", note, row=name, detail=detail)
    return "%-13s %-6s registry emit ok=%s %s" % ("  ->", "alarm", ok, note)


def clear_row_alarm(name, dry):
    """Close this row's registry row now that it reads `up` again. Returns the report line, or
    None when there was nothing to close — a row that never alarmed costs no shim call at all."""
    open_rows = load_row_alarms()
    if name not in open_rows:
        return None
    if dry:
        return "%-13s %-6s would clear %s from the alarm registry, but --dry-run" % (
            "  ->", "clear", row_signature_class(name))
    row = open_rows[name]
    ok, cleared, note = clear_alarm(name, row.get("subject") or ROW_UNITS.get(name, name),
                                    row.get("signature_class") or row_signature_class(name))
    if ok:
        open_rows.pop(name, None)
        save_row_alarms(open_rows)
        ledger("alarm-cleared", "the row reads `up` again, so its standing condition is over",
               row=name, cleared=cleared, emitter=note)
    else:
        ledger("alarm-clear-failed", note, row=name)
    return "%-13s %-6s registry clear ok=%s %s" % ("  ->", "clear", ok, note)


# ── notify ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def slack_post(method, token, body):
    req = urllib.request.Request(
        "https://slack.com/api/" + method,
        data=json.dumps(body).encode(),
        headers=dict(UA, authorization="Bearer " + token))
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=TIMEOUT) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read())


def owner_dm_user():
    """Resolved from the chat bridge's OWN config, with the bridge's own fallback
    (explicit dm user, else the first allowlist entry), so the watchdog and the
    bridge can never disagree about who 'the owner' is."""
    path = env("IGNITE_CHAT_BRIDGE_CONFIG")
    if not path:
        return None
    with open(path) as f:
        cfg = json.load(f)
    allow = cfg.get("allowlist") or []
    return cfg.get("bus_ferry_dm_user") or (allow[0] if allow else None)


def notify(text, dry=False):
    """Its OWN two-call Slack client, deliberately: this must reach the owner when
    the chat bridge is the thing that is down, so it can depend on neither the
    bridge process nor its bus-poll loop. Returns (ok, detail)."""
    text = (env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_PREFIX", "") + text).strip()
    # BEFORE any sink, because ok=True would let a dry run consume the dedupe state.
    # A dry run SHOWS the message and is structurally incapable of sending it — the
    # `alarm` rows reach here without passing the --dry-run branch in main(), so the
    # guard has to sit on the send itself, not on the path that leads to it.
    if dry:
        return False, "--dry-run: NOT sent. Would have sent:\n" + text
    sink = env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_NOTIFY_FILE")
    if sink:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(sink)), exist_ok=True)
        with open(sink, "a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps({"at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime()),
                                "text": text}) + "\n")
        return True, "written to %s" % sink
    token = env("SLACK_BOT_TOKEN")
    if not token:
        return False, "no SLACK_BOT_TOKEN"
    try:
        user = owner_dm_user()
        if not user:
            return False, "no owner DM user resolvable from IGNITE_CHAT_BRIDGE_CONFIG"
        opened = slack_post("conversations.open", token, {"users": user})
        if not opened.get("ok"):
            return False, "conversations.open failed: %s" % opened.get("error")
        sent = slack_post("chat.postMessage", token,
                          {"channel": opened["channel"]["id"], "text": text})
        if not sent.get("ok"):
            return False, "chat.postMessage failed: %s" % sent.get("error")
        return True, "ts=%s" % sent.get("ts")
    except Exception as ex:
        return False, "%s: %s" % (type(ex).__name__, ex)


def state_path():
    return env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_STATE",
               os.path.join(WORKSPACE, ".rbtv", "runtime", "watchdog", "state.json"))


def load_state():
    try:
        with open(state_path()) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except Exception:
        return {}


def save_state(fingerprint, since=None, notified_at=None):
    """`since` = when THIS fingerprint first alerted; `notified_at` = when the owner
    was last actually told. The gate reads notified_at (so a re-alert does not then
    fire every pass); the message reads since (so it can say how long this has stood).

    ⚠ `at` is refreshed on EVERY alerting pass, notified or not (R1): since an alarm no
    longer paints the unit failed, THIS FILE is the standing-alarm signal, and a reader
    must be able to tell an alarm still standing this minute from one last seen 6h ago."""
    now = time.time()
    write_json_atomic(state_path(), {"alert": fingerprint,
                          "since": since if since is not None else now,
                          "notified_at": now if notified_at is None else notified_at,
                          "at": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(now))})


def main(argv):
    targets = [t.strip() for t in env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_TARGETS", ",".join(ROWS)).split(",") if t.strip()]
    dry = False
    for a in argv:
        if a == "--dry-run":
            dry = True
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print("usage: rbtv-ignite-watchdog [--dry-run]\n")
            print(__doc__)
            return 0
        else:
            print("usage: rbtv-ignite-watchdog [--dry-run]", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
    if WORKSPACE is None:
        # THE REFUSAL, at the run boundary and not at import: the probes that import this file as a
        # module set `W.WORKSPACE` themselves, and a module-level exit would kill them on import.
        print("refusing: no RBTV_WATCHDOG_WORKSPACE and the cwd %s does not root an ignite install "
              "(no %s) — a watchdog that invents a workspace watches a directory nothing writes to"
              % (os.getcwd(), INSTALL_RECORD_REL), file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    unknown = [t for t in targets if t not in ROWS]
    if unknown:
        print("unknown target(s): %s (known: %s)" % (", ".join(unknown), ", ".join(ROWS)),
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    lines, alerts, dry_down, alarming = [], [], [], []
    for name in targets:
        probe, restart, describe = ROWS[name]
        state, detail = probe()
        lines.append("%-13s %-6s %s" % (name, state, detail))
        if state in ("up", "skip"):
            if name == "daemon" and state == "up" and not dry:
                clear_failcount()
            # `up` ONLY, never `skip`: a skipped row was not GRADED, and closing a standing
            # condition because nobody looked is the absence-reads-as-health shape this whole
            # component exists to remove.
            if state == "up":
                cleared = clear_row_alarm(name, dry)
                if cleared:
                    lines.append(cleared)
            continue
        if state == "alarm":
            # ⚠ NO `alerts.append` HERE — see the ROW_UNITS block above. This is ONE delivery,
            # through the emitter, and the standing condition then lives in the alarm registry
            # that `ignite status` and the system digest both read.
            alarming.append(name)
            lines.append(raise_row_alarm(name, detail, dry))
            continue
        if dry:
            # A dry run INSPECTS. It restarts nothing and it must also not DM: a
            # diagnostic verb with a side effect on the owner's inbox is a verb
            # nobody dares run.
            lines.append("%-13s %-6s would restart, but --dry-run" % ("  ->", "dry"))
            dry_down.append(name)
            continue
        if name == "daemon":
            # The ACTION path is the only place this can sit: the 6h repeat-suppression below
            # gates the DM leg, which is structurally downstream of the restart and can
            # therefore never throttle an act.
            allow, note = daemon_restart_gate(detail)
            if not allow:
                lines.append("%-13s %-6s %s" % ("  ->", "held", note))
                alerts.append(note)
                continue
        rc, out = restart()
        after, after_detail = probe()
        # The other half of the T4-R9 duty: a restart TAKEN is a decision too, and its reprobe
        # verdict is what tells a later reader whether the remedy worked. Every row, not only the
        # daemon: `bridge` and `probe-suite` restart through the same lever.
        ledger("restart-taken", describe(), row=name, rc=rc, before=detail,
               reprobe=after, reprobe_detail=after_detail)
        lines.append("%-13s %-6s restart rc=%s | reprobe=%s %s"
                     % ("  ->", "acted", rc, after, after_detail))
        if after in ("up", "skip"):
            alerts.append("%s was down (%s) — %s, and it is back." % (name, detail, describe()))
        else:
            alerts.append("%s is DOWN and did NOT come back after %s — %s. Restart output: %s"
                          % (name, describe(), after_detail, out[-400:] or "<none>"))

    # The four systemd-sourced verdicts (RESTARTED · CRASH-LOOP · IDENTITY · stale code).
    # Outside the restart table on purpose: no restart fixes any of them, and the `daemon`
    # row above already owns the only restart lever this unit has. A --dry-run reads them
    # but persists nothing — consuming the prior-pass identity would swallow the very
    # restart the next real pass exists to announce.
    if "daemon" in targets:
        rows, notes = daemon_verdicts(persist=not dry)
        lines.extend(rows)
        if not dry:
            alerts.extend(notes)

    print("\n".join(lines))

    if alerts:
        # ponytail: fingerprint dedupe with a re-alert ceiling, not a rate limiter. A 60s
        # cadence against a subject that cannot be restored would DM the owner ~1440x/day —
        # CMP-28 invariant 3 violated by volume rather than by rule. But dedupe ALONE fails
        # the other way: a permanently stuck condition (a token never minted, a subject that
        # never comes back) sends one DM and then goes silent, which is indistinguishable
        # from a healthy system on the only channel that pushes. So an UNCHANGED alert is
        # suppressed for at most REALERT, then re-sent saying how long it has stood.
        realert = float(env("RBTV_WATCHDOG_REALERT_SECONDS", "21600"))
        fingerprint = " | ".join(sorted(a.split(" —")[0].split(" (")[0] for a in alerts))
        last = load_state()
        repeat = fingerprint == last.get("alert")
        prior_notified = float(last.get("notified_at") or 0)
        silent_for = time.time() - prior_notified
        since = last.get("since") if repeat else None
        notified_at = prior_notified if repeat else 0
        if repeat and silent_for < realert:
            print("\n[repeat] unchanged since the previous pass — not re-notifying for another %dm: %s"
                  % (int((realert - silent_for) / 60), fingerprint))
        else:
            head = "*ignite watchdog*"
            if repeat:
                head += "  — STILL UNRESOLVED, first alerted %dm ago" % int(
                    (time.time() - float(since or time.time())) / 60)
            ok, detail = notify(head + "\n" + "\n".join("• " + a for a in alerts), dry=dry)
            print("\n[notify] ok=%s %s" % (ok, detail))
            if ok:
                notified_at = time.time()
        # R1: the exit code no longer says an alarm is standing, so THE STATE FILE HAS TO —
        # written on every alerting pass, including a suppressed repeat and a notify that
        # FAILED (that pass is the one where the owner was not told, so it is the last one
        # that may leave no record). `notified_at` moves only when he actually was.
        # A --dry-run still persists nothing: it must not consume a real pass's record.
        if not dry:
            save_state(fingerprint, since, notified_at)
    elif dry_down:
        # Not green, but nothing was done and nothing is claimed. The dedupe state is
        # deliberately left untouched: a dry run must not consume a real pass's
        # "already told the owner about this" record.
        print("\n--dry-run: %d subject(s) down (%s) — nothing restarted, no DM sent"
              % (len(dry_down), ", ".join(dry_down)))
    elif alarming:
        # NOT "all green", and NOT a DM. The condition stands, it is carried by the alarm registry
        # (which the system digest and `ignite status` read), and saying "all green" on this pass
        # is the exact sentence that made the wave-test-2 answer wrong.
        if load_state().get("alert") is not None:
            save_state(None)
        print("\n%d standing alarm(s) — %s — carried by the alarm registry, not by a DM: "
              "read them with `ignite status` (open_conditions) or the 2-hourly system digest"
              % (len(alarming), ", ".join(alarming)))
    else:
        if load_state().get("alert") is not None:
            save_state(None)
        print("\nall green — exiting silently (no DM)")

    # R1: a pass that RAN is exit 0, whatever it found. Nonzero is reserved for the
    # watchdog itself breaking (an unhandled exception, or the usage errors above), so
    # `systemctl --user --failed` means "the watchdog is broken" and nothing else.
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
